  "question": {
    "text": "Let the point $(-1, \\alpha, \\beta)$ lie on the line of the shortest distance between the lines $\\frac{x+2}{-3}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-5}{2}$ and $\\frac{x+2}{-1}=\\frac{y+6}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{0}$. Then $(\\alpha-\\beta)^2$ is equal to___________",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "25",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/11PT6YQUyi-liU4vS2EEUaSdfY6IMneNlEsFgn4dKB8.original.fullsize.png\"><br />$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{P}(-3 \\lambda-2,4 \\lambda+2,2 \\lambda+5) \\\\\n& \\mathrm{Q}(-\\mu-2,2 \\mu-6,1) \\\\\n& \\mathrm{DRS} \\text { of } \\mathrm{PQ}=(3 \\lambda-\\mu, 2 \\mu-4 \\lambda-8,-2 \\lambda-4) \\\\\n& \\mathrm{DRS} \\text { of } \\mathrm{PQ}=\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\n\\hat{\\mathrm{i}} & \\hat{\\mathrm{j}} & \\hat{\\mathrm{k}} \\\\\n-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\\n-3 & 4 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right| \\\\\n& =(4 \\hat{\\mathrm{i}}+2 \\hat{\\mathrm{j}}+2 \\hat{\\mathrm{k}})\n\\end{aligned}$\nOR\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& (2,1,1) \\\\\n& \\frac{3 \\lambda-\\mu}{2}=\\frac{2 \\mu-4 \\lambda-8}{1}=\\frac{-2 \\lambda-4}{1} \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\mu=\\lambda+2 \\& 7 \\lambda=\\mu-8 \\\\\n& \\lambda=-1 \\quad \\mu=1 \\\\\n& Q:(-3,-4,1) \\\\\n& L_{P Q}=\\frac{x+3}{2}=\\frac{y+4}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\\n& (-1, \\alpha, \\beta) \\Rightarrow 1=\\frac{\\alpha+4}{1}=\\frac{\\beta-1}{1} \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\alpha=-3, \\beta=2 \\\\\n& (\\alpha-\\beta)^2=25\n\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
